Informed people make better decisions — studies of financial literacy, health literacy, and civic knowledge consistently show that more knowledgeable individuals achieve better outcomes across all life domains.
The ability to distinguish reliable from unreliable information — a knowledge-dependent skill — has never been more valuable, as misinformation proliferates and the consequences of false belief escalate.
Socrates' claim that 'no one does wrong willingly' implies that wrongdoing stems from ignorance — a radical position that, if correct, makes knowledge the foundation of ethics.
